{"id":"gifts-cover-against-list","title":"Cover pages whose totals differ from the gifts itemised under them","question":"Does the total printed on each cover page match the gifts the same filing itemises?","finding":"Of 318 department-months within the period the itemised list covers, 271 match their itemised gifts in count and value (within 1%, counting the original alone or with its amendments) and 47 do not; in 1 the cover pages report gifts and the list holds none for that month. The largest gap: Recreation and Parks (REC), 2026-05, cover $750,632 against $577 itemised. In 39 of the 47, the gifts itemised equal one amendment alone. In 2 of those the amendment is a correction and nothing is missing (Public Health (DPH), 2025-02; District Attorney (DAT), 2025-10): its explanation says it removes gifts from the original, to be reported in a later month, or that the originals were sent twice. In the other 37 the original filing's gifts, $2.5 million on the cover pages, are missing from the published list.","how":"Cover pages are grouped by filing series (department and month) and set against the gifts itemised under the same series in the list downloaded 12 November 2026. Only series already published on 12 November and whose month ends by the list's last gift date are tested.","limits":"A cover page with zero gifts and no items is a match. Differences can come from rounding, from an amendment filed under a new envelope, from in-kind gifts valued later, or from the portal splitting one filing; the signed PDF linked on each row is the record. A mismatch is a data question for the Ethics Commission, not a finding about the department: where the list holds only an amendment, the published dataset looks wrong, not the filing, and the signed PDF of the original should hold the missing gifts. Every count of named donors built on the list (the site’s “Side doors: department gifts, commissioners, ballot committees, enforcement” page included) misses those gifts.","tier":"R